Declarative homed user records, for a host that is already running.

To declare a user BEFORE the host has ever booted, do not use this directory
— drop a `home.create.<name>.cred` (homed) or `sysusers.extra.cred` (classic)
into `loader/credentials/` on the target's ESP instead. That is the only path
that works on a disk `systemd-repart` has not yet initialised, because this
directory lives on the /etc overlay, which lives on data-luks, which does not
exist until first boot. See credentials/README.md in the myosi repo.

On a running host there are two ways in:

    myosi user-create <name> homed 1000 wheel,video,render,input,kvm

creates the record, the LUKS home with the correct storage flags, the group
bindings declared by active sysexts, and the /etc/subuid + /etc/subgid ranges.
That is the normal path.

Or drop <name>.user here and let myosi-users.service pick it up on the next
boot. user-provision searches this directory first, then the baked
/usr/share/myosi/users/. This one is writable and survives A/B image updates;
/usr is read-only erofs, so records cannot be added there.

(An earlier myosi-homed-user@<name>.service is gone — its work moved into the
`myosi user-create` recipe and myosi-users.service.)

The file is the "regular" section of a systemd user record. See homectl(1) and
https://systemd.io/USER_RECORD/. A record handed to homed as a credential can
additionally carry the storage flags inline — storage, fileSystemType,
diskSize, luksDiscard, luksOfflineDiscard, autoResizeMode,
luksExtraMountOptions — which is how the credential path reproduces what the
helper applies here.

Example — /etc/myosi/users/alan.user:

    {
        "userName": "alan",
        "uid": 1000,
        "gid": 1000,
        "realName": "Alan Example",
        "homeDirectory": "/home/alan",
        "shell": "/usr/bin/fish",
        "memberOf": ["wheel", "video", "render", "input", "kvm"],
        "preferredLanguage": "en_US.UTF-8",
        "service": "io.systemd.Home",
        "enforcePasswordPolicy": false
    }

The home is created with the bootstrap password `changeme`; rotate it with
`homectl passwd <name>`.

Notes:
  - uid 1000 marks the primary user. If that uid is already taken, the helper
    skips creation and binds groups to the existing owner instead.
  - Creation is one-shot, gated on `homectl inspect`. Editing this file later
    does not re-create or reshape an existing home.
